{
  "concept": "Graphing two linear equations on shared axes turns an algebraic system into a picture: the single point where the lines cross is the ordered pair that satisfies both equations at once.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Dotted square coordinate grid spanning x from -6 to 6 and y from -5 to 5, one unit per cell",
    "Horizontal x-axis, black, with arrowheads at both ends and integer tick labels -6 through 6",
    "Vertical y-axis, black, with arrowheads at both ends and integer tick labels -5 through 5",
    "Axis names: italic 'x' at the right end of the horizontal axis, italic 'y' at the top of the vertical axis",
    "Blue line of negative slope (slope -1/2) crossing the y-axis just below the origin at -1/2, entering upper-left and exiting lower-right, arrowheads at both ends",
    "Blue equation label at the far left of the blue line reading 'x + 2y = -1'",
    "Red line of positive slope (slope 1) crossing the y-axis at 3, rising from lower-left to upper-right, arrowheads at both ends",
    "Red equation label at the lower left reading '-x + y = 3'",
    "Filled black dot at the intersection in the second quadrant, just left of the y-axis and slightly above the x-axis",
    "Stacked-fraction coordinate annotation above the dot reading (-7/3, 2/3)",
    "Dashed black guide segments (appear on click) dropping from the intersection dot to the x-axis and across to the y-axis",
    "Substitution check panel (appears on click) showing each equation with the dot's coordinates substituted and both sides evaluated",
    "Status caption reporting 'one solution' / 'no solution — parallel lines' / 'solution off-grid'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "blueIntercept",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Blue line constant c in x + 2y = c",
      "range": [-8, 8, 0.5],
      "default": -1,
      "teaches": "Shifting one line up or down slides the crossing point along the other line — the solution is a location determined jointly by both equations, not by either alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "redSlope",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Red line slope m in y = m·x + 3",
      "range": [-2, 2, 0.1],
      "default": 1,
      "teaches": "Rotating a line about its y-intercept moves the crossing point, and when its slope matches the blue line's -1/2 the lines are parallel and the system has no solution."
    },
    {
      "id": "checkSolution",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Selecting the intersection dot substitutes its coordinates into both equations at once, showing the same ordered pair makes both statements true."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The original system",
      "narration": "Two lines are drawn on one grid: blue x + 2y = -1 falling to the right, red -x + y = 3 rising to the right. They cross exactly once, in the second quadrant.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": -1, "redSlope": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the solution",
      "narration": "Click the black dot: dashed guides drop to the axes at x = -7/3 and y = 2/3, and both equations check out when those values are substituted.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": -1, "redSlope": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide the blue line",
      "narration": "Change the constant to c = 3 and the blue line shifts up; the crossing point slides along the red line to a new place, but there is still exactly one solution.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": 3, "redSlope": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Rotate the red line",
      "narration": "Flatten the red line's slope toward -0.5 and watch the intersection race away to the left, off the visible grid — the solution still exists but is far from the origin.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": -1, "redSlope": -0.4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Parallel: no solution",
      "narration": "At slope exactly -0.5 the red line is parallel to the blue line. The lines never meet, so the system has no solution at all.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": -1, "redSlope": -0.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Back to one crossing",
      "narration": "Tilt the red line back to a positive slope and a single crossing reappears — orientation, not position, decides whether a solution exists.",
      "state": { "blueIntercept": -1, "redSlope": 1 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A solution to a system is not a number but a point: the one ordered pair that lies on both lines simultaneously — and if the lines are parallel, no such point exists.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square-aspect Cartesian plane on a light dotted grid, x from -6 to 6 and y from -5 to 5, black axes with arrowheads and italic x / y axis names. Blue (#1a6b8f-ish teal-blue) line with slope -1/2 through y-intercept -1/2, labeled 'x + 2y = -1' in matching blue at the left edge near where the line leaves the grid. Red line with slope 1 through y-intercept 3, labeled '-x + y = 3' in red at the lower left. Both lines drawn with arrowheads at both visible ends. A solid black dot marks the intersection just left of the y-axis, slightly above the x-axis, with a stacked-fraction label (-7/3, 2/3) set above and to the right of it. Equation labels must update live as the sliders change (e.g. 'x + 2y = 3', '-x + y = 3' rewritten as slope-intercept when rotated), and the coordinate label recomputes to exact fractions where possible. When the lines are parallel, hide the dot and show a caption 'No solution — parallel lines'; when the intersection leaves the grid, keep the caption 'Solution off-grid' with an arrow at the grid edge pointing toward it. Click state adds dashed grey drop-lines to both axes and a small substitution panel below the plot echoing each equation with values plugged in and both sides evaluated in the line's own color."
}

